 "title": "Same 1 Million",
 "note": "v3: Hakeem on camera (Retirement Scenes) + spoken CTA from the How-will-you-create-income recording; times on the final video timeline.",
 "text": "Two retirees, same $1 million, the same $60 ,000 a year to live on. One of them can run out of money, the other never will. Here's the difference. Meet James. He puts all of it into the S &P 500 fund, VOO, and takes out 6 % a year. James retires right into a rough market, down 15%, then down 10, then down 5. But his bills don't stop, so he needs to keep selling even at the bottom. In three years, his million is down to $575 ,000. meet Carol, the same million, the same $60 ,000 a year, same bad start, but this time she buys income first. She puts $500 ,000 into a private pension account paying 6 .5%. That's $32 ,500 every year for the rest of her life, no matter what the market does. But if the markets were to average a little less, about 6 .6%, James runs out of money completely. In year 26, Carol, she still has money invested, and her $32 ,500 shows up every year for the rest of her life. That's the sequence of returns risk. When you're living off of your money, a bad start can break your plan, even if the averages look fine. James is hoping that the market cooperates. Carol has a paycheck. So if you're someone who never thought about how to get income after you stop working, comment, PLAN, and I'll reach out to you personally,",
